  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Cold mornings with valley fog for much of the rest of the week. \n\n- A warming trend through the end of the week with Friday 10 to 15\n  degrees above average. \n\n- Cold trough and cold front coming for early next week, though\n  most of the precipitation will probably got further south. \n\nA cold, stable air mass left behind the last cold front to come\nthrough will make for some cool mornings at high elevations and in\nvalleys where it stays clear for a good portion of the night. A\nhard freeze is expected in those areas. But satellite is showing\nsome of the residual moisture and melting snow is causing low\nstratus clouds to form, which are keeping the overnight lows up in\nmuch of western Montana and the Clearwater and Nez Perce mountains\nof central Idaho. \n\nThe stable conditions will continue as a ridge builds over the\nregion. Today is the last day of the week that temperatures will\nbe below average, and by Friday they'll be 10 to 15 degrees above\naverage for this time of year. These stable conditions will make\nit a challenge to ventilate out the valleys and get rid of all the\nstratus by the end of the week, so most valleys are only expected\nto cool into the mid-20s to low 30s most of the rest of the week. \n\nEnsembles continue to show another trough and cold front coming\nacross the region early next week. About 60 percent of ensembles\nshow an open wave and the rest of the models show a cutoff low\ndiving to the south. All the models are showing mild to modest\namounts of precipitation. The biggest difference in the patterns\nwould be where the snow level ends up, with the 60 percent\nsolution favoring lower snow levels.",
